With \(V_{DD}\) and both bias voltages at AC ground, \(M_2\) presents \(r_{o2}\) at node \(X\). The lower transistor \(M_1\) provides the cascode effect, giving

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
\;\approx\;g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}}
\]

where the approximation applies when the product term dominates. If body effect is included, replace \(g_{m1}\) with \(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\).

With \(V_{DD}\), \(V_{b1}\), and \(V_{b2}\) at AC ground, M₂ contributes \(r_{o2}\), while M₁ provides the cascode gain. Neglecting body effect,

\[
\boxed{R_{\text{out}}=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
\;\approx\;g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}}
\]

The approximation holds when the \(g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}\) term dominates.
